@node Threads
@chapter Threads

libdtfl keeps almost no global state, and the state it does keep lives
in a worker that belongs to one thread.  The full contract is in
@code{dtfl(7)}; it has six rules.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Handles belong to one worker.}  A @code{dtfl_worker} and
everything reached from it (scopes, streams, collections, operators,
capabilities, input handles, probes and traces) belong to the thread
that runs that worker.  Using one from another thread is undefined
behavior.  Debug builds (@option{--enable-debug}) detect the violation
and call the panic hook.  This matches Rust, where those types are
@code{!Send}.

@item
@strong{The thread-safe exceptions.}  A few objects may be used from any
thread: @code{dtfl_sync_activator} (to wake a worker from outside), a
read-only shared probe snapshot, the synchronized event link, the
@code{dtfl_guards} join handle, and the four global hook setters.  The
setters must be called before the first @code{dtfl_execute}.

@item
@strong{Inter-worker data moves only through the allocator's queues.}
Record batches are reference counted with atomic counts, because they
cross threads in the multi-worker communication modes.

@item
@strong{Trace sharing is worker-local.}  A trace (the shared index
behind an arrangement) imports only into dataflows on the same worker.
Rust forbids cross-worker sharing (@code{TraceAgent} is an @code{Rc}),
and so does libdtfl.

@item
@strong{No hidden global state.}  The only process-wide writable state is
the four host hooks (allocation, panic, logging, thread spawning); the
only per-thread state is the buffer behind
@code{dtfl_last_error_detail}.  The library installs no signal handlers,
reads no environment variables, and touches no @code{getopt} globals.
Two independent @code{dtfl_execute} calls can therefore run at the same
time in one process.

@item
@strong{Reentrancy.}  An operator callback may call any API on its own
worker @emph{except} @code{dtfl_worker_step}; a nested step is detected
and calls the panic hook.
@end enumerate

The contract is verified by a ThreadSanitizer build over the
multi-worker suites, by Helgrind on the typed-process allocator, and by
a symbol-table check that fails on any writable or thread-local symbol
in the shared library outside the allowlisted hook and error-buffer
globals.

@section Placing workers on cores and sockets

libdtfl does not pin its worker threads.  A host that wants placement
gives it through the thread hooks: its @code{spawn} callback starts the
thread and sets its affinity before calling @code{start}, or the program
runs under @command{taskset} or @command{numactl}.  Worker @var{i}'s
thread is the @var{i}-th spawned; network threads follow the workers.

Measured on a two-socket c6i.metal (2 x 32 cores, clock pinned at
2.9 GHz, the pinned @code{arrange} benchmark, 16 workers):

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .25 .25 .20
@headitem Placement @tab C records/s @tab Rust records/s @tab C slower by
@item packed (one socket) @tab 69.4M @tab 93.2M @tab 1.34x
@item spread (alternating) @tab 56.3M @tab 78.3M @tab 1.39x
@end multitable

Spreading costs both builds about the same, a fifth of their
throughput, because exchanged data and progress messages cross the
socket interconnect.  Pack workers onto one socket while they fit, and
spread only when one socket's cores or memory bandwidth are not
enough.  @command{dev/bench-ec2 --placement packed|spread} reruns the
comparison.
